#uuid "5c1d99ed-7ae8-5b15-8a08-bbeeb0e40298" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530285} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b4c2d2-d6c4-51da-8c30-677fe144cd8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529749} {:answer/id #uuid "4bed7233-96d2-578f-a21c-52d27abdb309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529761} {:answer/id #uuid "e1182578-b270-52e2-8352-fdfedca769cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530143} {:answer/id #uuid "e82f4eee-7f0c-5716-aa04-bc8bc484efbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529761} :answer-field/id #uuid "79f3c88c-fd69-59e2-8a96-4be51318ef16" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530312} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a274b0c-6c62-5d46-b00f-51fcc759f2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "phase portrait" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529632} {:answer/id #uuid "6e77e88c-151b-511e-ba46-7e88b0ad53cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "vector field" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529632} :answer-field/id #uuid "a950cdfa-506c-5334-8ecd-ea28ae6589d6" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e228f7c-6904-5be1-bb24-073dee51f110" :question/math-academy-id "q-332486" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/e0/e0446b7851fe0f0612d3db67de05188c0ed63af41ff1dfe2773158b2382fe722.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line\n\nSince ${e}^{t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa12152c9882d0953667f26e888d0fbac375f4cc0c348f3710799e1a6e22349.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/83/83885d091455a8f9b9a1c33556d9deeaee550d50dd665e016c2e8072c926f41a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/e0/e0446b7851fe0f0612d3db67de05188c0ed63af41ff1dfe2773158b2382fe722.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa12152c9882d0953667f26e888d0fbac375f4cc0c348f3710799e1a6e22349.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/83/83885d091455a8f9b9a1c33556d9deeaee550d50dd665e016c2e8072c926f41a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90771919-fe3a-5feb-8f43-29a2eba3450c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164382} {:answer/id #uuid "95cc0d88-f4f6-52b8-bff7-815d9494543e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186164383} :answer-field/id #uuid "58d47f9d-027f-5655-9b04-aa092645db06" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "965806e1-be1a-5edc-97dc-84aff040e3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164385} {:answer/id #uuid "fb61e42f-7627-523b-8977-123ee7e44576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186164386} :answer-field/id #uuid "857925f5-19ce-57b7-9662-2aa8862cf264" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00e880d4-f09c-5b80-8d65-2954cf7b3f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-332768" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fe96278d09a5875c2f1e7c8762a808fd9549b37508f70b129b20e311b166308.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8716e607-f4af-5764-b0f4-37fea7ca8f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164470} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f4c26e-b8e1-50ac-8f1c-7401387c76b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a42df9a876ef99a68ecb211a691211f7398c71d32426c55ce906171f1e563692.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164471} {:answer/id #uuid "a70dc086-4d01-5f96-9237-82d92d5496e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2a8375ddd5ab11a79bda95a0f1dc51daf62bc452a3ffec8347227f069bc7bd57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164472} {:answer/id #uuid "b60a1882-b0f8-5e3d-9e59-9e94e2f5bd1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164473} {:answer/id #uuid "5b93229b-5f6f-571e-b668-8e3e26328d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e4bad1b9f0d5b0621fdbd7caf5278018ccbff5a60ede579f591ffbeacd7f741c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186164471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7252f43-4dd1-594c-840b-b764aff0a2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39b2d4dd-f57e-54ad-a67c-f1f62018e20b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333187" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2196ea04e12f3c706300e0c9d15806606e408f5e582d3e4f2b9c62f0efc343f.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/14/1429eda4336d47aec06c7dd673ff6163203423739c520fd0c83db1c5cb072ac7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883da431-7bbd-57a3-bb9a-04014b80e4d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164548} {:answer/id #uuid "c93dac15-9320-5857-ac4b-f31b40d7838a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164549} {:answer/id #uuid "e10c483a-ea8c-5386-b63f-0b0afb0a6384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 