:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556478} :answer-field/id #uuid "f267bf00-331a-5ba4-80d1-f50c27b73d21" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "370d8409-9b53-5d37-9911-f67945640175" :question/math-academy-id "q-334651" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"solid\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x-3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-3xy)=2-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}2-3y & -3x \\\\ 4y & -5+4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2-3(0) & -3(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -5+4(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "920b1022-bc85-5441-9714-70e679a31b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164672} {:answer/id #uuid "af366199-e291-55b1-a9e6-f8155d482a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/m