k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561848}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561635} :answer-field/id #uuid "73aec003-f39a-520d-adb3-80d245644c7f" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f98673ec-d1ca-550a-94d6-d4c7a722e79f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334877" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-4x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"solid\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-4x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=2-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}2-16x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2-16(0)-(0) & -(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -2+4(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bfdb40-b020-5871-af7f-669ab81b634f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539484} {:answer/id #uuid "0d477725-039a-569b-974c-cc8168427bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539506} {:answer/id #uuid "133cc9fe-58b4-5563-9de2-f20044a1ad82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539516} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7a4ec2-f370-5681-81e3-c63a1d8a2f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539719} {:answer/id #uuid "a721105a-d718-5aac-86a9-455b5f022623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539484} :answer-field/id #uuid "163adf1f-a5ba-53ac-95f1-d113394667f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "487df78e-b6e4-5124-9586-cb2ed967b312" :question/math-academy-id "q-338784" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593a5576-438c-5a88-b1ab-4d454dbfe49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312688} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20e797-c5ce-5f0c-aa2b-9769c0a817b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} {:answer/id #uuid "914eefbb-7b51-5a10-b982-aac4cf2c32e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313133} {:answer/id #uuid "ce996b3c-559e-5ad2-a307-7344e79bfbdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313589} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d13f9-28a0-54cd-893d-2c8b3f144b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} :answer-field/id #uuid "597899e8-1ef3-5f83-bfcf-33d3f5ac1fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fcf50127-6a15-5bfa-b137-f8e9738bae25" :question/math-academy-id "q-340794" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}-2(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
